<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 10px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # [Insert Theme Number Here] – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 5px 0 0 0;"><i>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</i></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 5th Grade (CEFR: A1.3)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Helping Around the House</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> I Wash the Dishes Everyday.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Writing: Productive: Develop short poems or rhymes.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Interactive: Summarize feedback from peer reviews.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Writing: Can write a short poem or rhyme about household chores using simple vocabulary and repetition (e.g., "I sweep the floor, I wash the plate, I clean the room, I can't be late!").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can write a simple summary of peer feedback on a classmate's poem or rhyme about chores (e.g., "Maria's rhyme was fun. She used good words and talked about cleaning the house.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can organize peer feedback into simple categories (e.g., what they liked, "Good job: used rhyming words." and what can be improved, "Needs work: add more lines about chores.").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 15px 0 10px 0;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 1</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows pictures of different household chores (e.g., washing dishes, sweeping the floor, dusting the table) and asks students to identify them orally. Students then quickly write down one chore they do at home. This activates prior vocabulary and connects to the theme "I Wash the Dishes Everyday."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models writing a simple sentence about a chore using the present simple and a frequency adverb. For example, "I <b style="color:#e74c3c;">wash</b> the dishes <b style="color:#e74c3c;">every day</b>." The teacher explicitly models the pronunciation of /w/ in "wash" and /s/ in "sweep" while writing, drawing attention to how these words sound. The teacher then models how to write a simple rhyming sentence, e.g., "I <b style="color:#e74c3c;">sweep</b> the <b style="color:#e74c3c;">floor</b>, it's never a <b style="color:#e74c3c;">bore</b>!"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say 'I wash,' does it mean I wash now or often?" (Expected answer: Often/every day)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Do 'floor' and 'bore' sound the same at the end?" (Expected answer: Yes, they rhyme)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">8 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 2</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher presents a short, simple rhyming poem about household chores written on a large chart paper or projected. The poem includes vocabulary like "dishes," "floor," "clean," "wash," "sweep," and grammar like "I wash," "I sweep," "every day."</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Material:</b> A short, illustrated rhyming poem (e.g., "I wash the dishes, clean the plates, / I sweep the floor, before it's too late! / I help my family every day, / Chores are important, come what may!"). Genre: Short rhyming poem. Register: Simple and informal. Format: Written text with pictures.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Sociolinguistic: Discussing routines and describing daily tasks according to context.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                        <ul style="list-style-type: circle; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions:</b> "What is this poem about?" "Does the person in the poem like chores?"</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> Students circle all the chore verbs (wash, sweep, clean) and chore nouns (dishes, plates, floor, family) they find in the poem.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 3</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Writing)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Grammar: Present simple (e.g., "I wash," "He washes," "I don't like to wash the dishes."), Frequency adverbs (e.g., "every day," "often," "usually"). Vocabulary: Nouns (dishes, floor, kitchen, family, table), Verbs (wash, clean, sweep, help, dust, mop).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Cloze Sentences):</b> The teacher distributes a worksheet with cloze sentences about chores. Students complete the sentences by choosing the correct chore verb or noun from a word bank. (e.g., "I ______ the clothes every day." (fold/mop), "My mom ______ the floor." (sweeps/cooks)). This reinforces vocabulary and present simple.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice (Sentence Builders):</b> Students are given sentence starters and a list of verbs, nouns, and frequency adverbs. They write their own complete sentences about chores, practicing different combinations. (e.g., "I often...", "He usually...", "We always..."). Students must use at least two frequency adverbs and four chore words from the provided list in their sentences. E.g., "I often <b style="color:#e74c3c;">help</b> my mom to <b style="color:#e74c3c;">clean</b> the <b style="color:#e74c3c;">kitchen</b> <b style="color:#e74c3c;">every day</b>."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Check (Written Sentence Review):</b> Students work in pairs to exchange their written sentences from activity 2. Each student reads their partner's sentences and checks for correct use of verbs, nouns, and frequency adverbs. They highlight one good sentence and one sentence that could be improved. This encourages peer interaction and early feedback practice, linking to the interactive objective.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 4</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Written product: Students write a short rhyming poem (3-4 lines) about household chores.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher reminds students of the example poem from Stage 2 and provides a simple sentence frame or word bank to guide their writing.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students are instructed to write a poem titled "My Chores," including at least three different chores and using present simple verbs and "every day" or "often." They should try to include rhyming words, though perfect rhyme is not the primary focus.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example sentence starters: "I _____ the _____ every day," "It is a big _____ for me," "I like to _____ and _____."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students are encouraged to use vocabulary from the unit (e.g., dishes, floor, kitchen, family, wash, clean, sweep, help). They focus on generating their own creative lines. The teacher circulates, providing support and checking for task completion.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 5</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment, reflection)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Peer assessment using a simple feedback form, followed by writing a summary of feedback.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students exchange their poems with a partner. Each pair receives a "Peer Feedback Form" with simple questions:
                        <ul style="list-style-type: circle; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What chores did your partner write about? (Write 2)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Did your partner use "every day" or "often"? (Yes/No)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What was good about the poem? (e.g., "Good job: used rhyming words," "Good words: clean and wash.")</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What can be improved? (e.g., "Needs work: add more lines about chores," "Needs work: check spelling for 'dishes'.")</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">After reviewing, each student writes a short, simple summary of the feedback they received on their own poem, organizing it into "What I liked" and "What can be improved." (e.g., "Maria liked my poem. She said it was fun. She said I need to add more chores.")</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (for poem & summary):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity of the poem (Is it easy to understand the chores?).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of target vocabulary (at least 3 chore words).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of present simple tense and frequency adverbs.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Effort in rhyming (if attempted).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity of summary (Can they identify positive and improvement areas?).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy about writing your poem today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was challenging or difficult?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 6</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Reflection</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Student and Teacher)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How did writing a poem help you practice describing chores?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new words or phrases about chores did you use in your poem today?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in teaching students to write poems/rhymes about chores? Were the model and sentence frames effective?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Did students successfully summarize peer feedback? What adjustments are needed for the next time we do peer review?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Were there any difficulties with grammar (present simple, frequency adverbs) or vocabulary that need further scaffolding?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In Lesson 5 (Mediation & Integration), students will present their poems and peer feedback summaries to the class. They will integrate their writing skills by creating a "Class Chores Book" or a similar 21st Century Skills Project, showing their individual poems and the feedback they received, along with illustrations.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">3 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>